## Codebase Patterns

Consolidated, reusable knowledge. Re-read in full every session -- keep
it tight.

- **Test scripts.** Since US-034 `npm run lint` is
  `standard && npm run lint:boundary`, and `npm test` is
  `npm run lint && depcheck && npm run test-unit`. Since US-019
  `npm run test-unit` = `npm run build:main && tape test/unit/*.js`:
  unit tests are CommonJS and require the Babel output under `build/`,
  never the ESM sources under `src/`. New files in `test/unit/` are
  globbed, no registration needed. Details in `test/AGENTS.md`.
- **`standard` cannot take custom rules.** Its `standard` key in
  `package.json` accepts only `ignore`, `globals`, `env`, `parser`,
  `plugins` and `settings` -- there is no `rules` escape hatch. Any
  project-specific rule therefore needs a second, standalone ESLint
  run. US-034's is `eslint-boundary.config.js` at the repo root, run
  by `npm run lint:boundary` with `--no-eslintrc` so the two configs
  never see each other. `eslint` (8.15.0) is now an explicit
  devDependency rather than a hoisted transitive of `standard`, and is
  in depcheck's `--ignores` list because it is only ever reached from
  an npm script. `standard-engine` sets `useEslintrc: false`, so a
  root ESLint config never leaks into a `standard` run -- but name the
  file something other than `.eslintrc.*` anyway.
- **A quoted `wt-` string in a *comment* fails the boundary scan.**
  `test/unit/platform-boundary.js` greps `/['"`]wt-/` over every
  renderer source outside `lib/platform/` and cannot tell code from
  prose -- backticks count as quotes. Write channel names bare in
  comments there.
- **`no-restricted-imports` sees `import` declarations only.** It does
  not see `require()`, which is the one deferral mechanism that
  actually works in this renderer (see the dynamic-`import()` entry
  below). So an import-specifier lint rule alone cannot close the
  boundary: the `require()` spellings, and every usage-level concern
  (`ipcRenderer.` member reads, `wt-` channel strings,
  `new remote.Menu`), stay in the source scan in
  `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`. The two are complementary --
  do not delete either believing the other covers it.
- **Test an ESLint config through the Node API, not by shelling out.**
  `new ESLint({ cwd, useEslintrc: false, overrideConfigFile })` plus
  `lintText(source, { filePath })` lints synthetic code at a path that
  need not exist, which is the only way to prove an `overrides`
  exemption actually exempts without dropping probe files into
  `src/`. `lintFiles()` on the real tree is the companion assertion.
  See `test/unit/lint-boundary.js`.
- **`npm run test-integration` is broken and was broken before this
  branch.** Spectron 19 cannot drive Electron 27 under the installed
  Node (v25): every run dies with `Error: Application not running` on
  the first test. Verified by stashing all changes and re-running at
  HEAD. Do not treat it as a gate; do not try to "fix" it as a side
  effect of another story. It also leaks a whole Electron process tree
  when it fails -- always `pkill -f "$PWD/node_modules/"` afterwards.
- **Babel config lives in the `babel` key of `package.json`**, not a
  `.babelrc`. It is `@babel/plugin-transform-react-jsx` with
  `useBuiltIns: true` (reuse those exact options anywhere JSX has to be
  compiled) plus, since US-005,
  `@babel/plugin-transform-modules-commonjs` with
  `sourceType: "unambiguous"`. Without the modules plugin Babel leaves
  `import`/`export` untouched and Electron 27 (Node 18) cannot
  `require()` the result -- the app dies at startup. `unambiguous`
  keeps the still-CommonJS files compiling as scripts instead of
  gaining `"use strict"`.
- **`tape test/unit/*.js` is one process that requires every file
  before running any test.** Anything process-wide leaks between test
  files in alphabetical glob order. Two known cases: `setDispatch()`
  from `lib/dispatcher` (register it inside each file's per-test
  `reset()`, not once at require time, or the last file loaded wins for
  the whole run) and `require.cache` stubs of **built shim modules**
  (`platform-ipc-os.js` tests the real `ipc-os`, so a stub left at its
  path fails it -- withdraw the stub right after requiring the module
  under test; Babel has already bound the module object). Symptom:
  "my new test file broke a file I never touched".
- **`src/config.js` exports every value twice** -- as a named export
  (for CommonJS `src/main/`) and inside `export default {...}` (for ESM
  renderer code). Add to both or the value is silently missing for half
  the codebase. Details in `src/AGENTS.md`.
- **`bin/clean.js`, `bin/package.js` and `bin/open-config.js` require
  `../src/config` from raw source**, so plain Node loads it as real
  ESM, not through Babel. CommonJS-only globals (`__dirname`,
  `require`) do not exist on that path. Nothing else in the repo
  notices when they break.
- **`node -e` leaks `__dirname` (`'.'`) into ESM modules it loads.**
  Probe module scope from a real `.cjs`/`.mjs` file instead, or you
  will chase paths that only look wrong.
- **`npm start` = `npm run build && electron --no-sandbox .`**, and
  since US-016 `npm run build` is two halves:
  `build:main` (Babel -> `build/`, with `src/renderer/main.js`,
  `components/`, `controllers/` and `pages/` ignored) then
  `build:renderer` (`vite build` -> `build-vite/`). Both must keep
  working at every commit (FR-2). The set Babel must still compile is
  fixed by the three non-Vite `require()` callers: `index.js` ->
  `build/main`, `static/webtorrent.html` ->
  `build/renderer/webtorrent.js`, and `src/main/index.js` ->
  `../renderer/lib/state` -- which is why `src/renderer/lib/**` is
  built by both. Details in `src/AGENTS.md`.
- **A new generated directory needs five registrations:** `.gitignore`,
  nodemon's `--ignore` list in the `watch` script, depcheck's
  `--ignore-dirs` in the `test` script, the `rimraf` block in
  `bin/package.js`, and `bin/clean.js`. Missing the nodemon one is the
  only failure that is silent at build time and breaks dev -- `watch`
  runs `--ext js,css`, so an emitted `.js` file restarts it forever.
- **`npm run package` cannot be run as-is in a session** -- `build()`
  in `bin/package.js` opens with `rimraf(node_modules)` + `npm ci`.
  Copy its `all` + `darwin` option objects into a throwaway script and
  call `electronPackager` directly instead; `prune: true` works on
  electron-packager's own temp copy and never touches the working
  `node_modules`. Then prove what shipped with
  `npx asar list app.asar` -- the `ignore` option is a 700-char
  alternation and reading it is not verification.
- **`material-ui` import shapes.** Every `material-ui/**` submodule the
  renderer uses is CJS ending in `.default`, so a plain default import
  is correct (`import Checkbox from 'material-ui/Checkbox'`). The one
  exception is `material-ui/styles/colors`, which sets
  `__esModule` and exports each colour by name with **no default** --
  it needs named imports (`import { grey400 } from ...`). A default
  import there silently yields `undefined` and the colour just goes
  missing.
- **Renderer modules still on `module.exports = X` import cleanly as
  default imports** under both Babel's interop and Rolldown, and
  multi-value ones (`lib/dispatcher`) as named imports. Converting a
  file to ESM therefore never requires touching its
  not-yet-converted dependencies.
- **`fn-getter`-wrapped lazy `require()` has no ESM equivalent.** When
  converting a module that lazy-loads siblings, replace the thunks with
  static imports and say so in the log; `import()` is async and does
  not fit the synchronous call sites. Check `fn-getter` is still used
  somewhere afterwards or depcheck fails. **But measure first** -- see
  the next two entries.
- **Dynamic `import()` never settles in the renderer.** No resolve, no
  reject, no console error, and control never returns to the caller
  (observed over a 60s launch). `static/main.html` loads
  `build/renderer/main.js` from a classic `<script>` in a `file://`
  document, so `import()` is Chromium's loader, not Node's. It is not
  an available deferral mechanism anywhere in `src/renderer/`, in
  Phase 2 or otherwise. A `require()` inside a function is the only
  one that works.
- **Hoisting a lazy `require()` to a static import can be expensive.**
  Cheap for most modules, but the casting stack in `lib/cast.js` costs
  3.85s of blocking load in the Electron renderer -- it takes startup
  from `init: 665ms` to `init: 4.5s`. A/B it: `git stash &&
  npm run build && npx electron --no-sandbox .` for the baseline
  number, and `performance.now()` either side of the `require` in
  `build/renderer/main.js` to locate the cost. "The app still launches"
  is not a startup measurement.
- **`delayedInit()` was dead code until US-027, and runs now.** The
  `electron.remote.getCurrentWindow()` call just above the
  `window.setTimeout(delayedInit, ...)` used to throw
  (`electron.remote` was removed in Electron 14), aborting the rest of
  `onState`. US-027 moved it onto `lib/platform/window.isVisible()`,
  so `checkDownloadPath()`, the visibility listener and
  `lazyLoadCast()` all run again. The visible consequence: the launch
  log gains a severity-3 `no such type:
  extensions.api.cast_channel.CastMessage` from `Cast.init()`. That is
  dormant code being reached, not a regression -- do not chase it as
  one, and do not treat it as a clean-launch failure.
- **There are two renderer `init:` lines in the launch log.** One from
  `webtorrent.js` (the hidden window) and, since US-027, one from
  `build-vite/renderer/main.js` -- `console.timeEnd('init')` in
  `main.js` was unreachable before. Use `a.slice(1, 5)` in the
  console-message hook so you capture `sourceId`, or you will
  attribute renderer output to the wrong file.
- **Native modals block the whole main process.** `showOpenDialogSync`
  / `showSaveDialogSync` freeze it, so in an appended probe block
  nothing after the dispatch that opens one ever runs -- no further
  `executeJavaScript`, no `setTimeout`, and `kill <pid>` does not
  land (`pkill -f "$PWD/node_modules/"` does). That silence is itself
  the evidence the dialog opened. Schedule a blocking probe LAST.
- **Verifying the app launches non-interactively:** run
  `npx electron --no-sandbox .` in the background, sleep ~12s, check
  the process is alive and that the log shows `'Main Window'` and
  `init: <time>`. Always kill the process afterwards. This is a launch
  check only -- it is NOT the six-step manual smoke test.
- **Vite 8 is Rolldown-based, not Rollup/esbuild.** Two consequences
  that will keep mattering: it handles CommonJS natively (no
  `@rollup/plugin-commonjs`, no CJS resolve errors), and Node builtins
  are externalized to throwing browser stubs with a *warning*, not an
  error. A green Vite build therefore proves very little on its own --
  check the warning stream, not just the exit code.
- **`electron` resolves silently to the wrong module.** Bundlers
  resolve `require('electron')` to `node_modules/electron/index.js`,
  the npm CLI helper that exports a path string, not the runtime API.
  No warning is emitted. Anything relying on `electron.ipcRenderer`
  will be `undefined` at runtime while the build reports success.
- **Renderer console output does NOT reach the terminal.** The
  `'Main Window'` lines in the launch log come from a `console.dir` in
  `src/main/ipc.js`, not from the renderer. To observe renderer console
  output non-interactively, patch the *build artifacts* (never the
  source -- `build/` is gitignored and regenerated by `npm run build`):
  add to `build/main/index.js`
  `app.on('web-contents-created', (e, wc) => wc.on('console-message',
  (...a) => console.log('RENDERER-CONSOLE:', JSON.stringify(a.slice(1,
  4)))))`, then launch, then `npm run build` to restore. Severity 3 in
  the first field means error. This is the only way to get real
  evidence for any "does X appear in the console" acceptance criterion.
- **Probing a renderer *module* non-interactively:** add a
  `window.__x = require('./lib/thing')` line to `src/renderer/main.js`,
  `npm run build`, append the probe to `build/main/index.js` (an HTTP
  server if the module needs one, plus a `wc.executeJavaScript(...)`
  whose promise resolves to a result string), launch, read the string
  off stdout, then restore the source and rebuild. Serve `Range`
  requests -- Chromium's media loader asks for them. This is how a
  "can the real webview decode/fetch it" criterion gets evidence.
- **The six-step manual smoke test cannot be run in a non-interactive
  session.** Do the launch check, do whatever targeted empirical probe
  the story allows, and then say plainly in `progress.log` that the
  smoke test was not run and which step carries the risk. Never claim
  it passed.
- **Probing the running renderer's DOM.** `src/renderer/main.js`
  exposes `window.state` and `window.dispatch`, so a temporary
  `app.on('web-contents-created', ...)` block appended to
  `build/main/index.js` can call
  `wc.executeJavaScript("window.dispatch('preferences')")` to navigate
  routes and read the DOM back non-interactively. Two caveats: the
  hidden webtorrent window also fires `did-finish-load` (it reports a
  ~53-char body and no `.header` -- not a failure), and torrent-list
  DOM metrics drift ~20% run to run as progress bars update. Compare
  stable anchors only: `.header` outerHTML and the preferences page.
- **Vite `resolve.alias` string `find` entries match as a prefix**, so
  aliasing a package name also captures its deep imports. Use
  `find: /^pkg-name$/` when only the bare specifier should be
  redirected. Related: throwaway Vite probe entries must live inside
  the repo (a `/tmp` entry cannot resolve `node_modules` and Rolldown
  reports it as a misleading unresolved-import error).
- **JSX-in-`.js` is handled by a hand-rolled plugin, not the `oxc`
  config key.** `vite.config.js` has a `webtorrent:jsx-in-js` plugin
  (`enforce: 'pre'`) that pipes this repo's own `src/**/*.js` through
  Vite's exported `transformWithOxc` with `lang: 'jsx'` and
  `jsx: { runtime: 'classic' }`. Setting `oxc: { lang: 'jsx' }`
  instead looks right and works in dev, but `vite build` delegates to
  the native `builtin:vite-transform` plugin, which derives the
  language from the file extension and ignores `lang`. Do not
  "simplify" the plugin back into the `oxc` key. Classic runtime is
  required because every component relies on the implicit
  `React.createElement` pragma.
- **Proving a specific file reached the bundle** when the output is
  minified: grep `build-vite/assets/main-*.js` for a string literal
  unique to that file. Identifiers are mangled; string literals are
  not.
- **A pre-existing renderer error is always in the launch log.**
  `src/renderer/main.js` calls `electron.remote.getCurrentWindow()`
  (lines 145, 406, 418), but `electron.remote` was removed from
  Electron, so every launch logs a severity-3 `Cannot read properties
  of undefined (reading 'getCurrentWindow')`. It is on master, not
  something you broke. `@electron/remote` is the working module.
  Whenever a launch check surfaces an error, `git stash` your diff,
  rebuild, and relaunch before believing it is yours.
- **Vite externals only work with `output.format: 'cjs'`.** Since
  US-014 the renderer bundle is CJS, which is what lets an emitted
  `require()` resolve against Electron's module registry under
  `nodeIntegration: true`. Verify the mechanism rather than the exit
  code: grep the output bundle for `require("` (should list every
  external) and for `^import|^export` (should be empty). Changing the
  format silently converts every external into a broken import.
- **The externalization warning count is the Node-builtin gate.**
  `grep -c 'has been externalized' <stderr>` should be 0. Anything
  above that is a builtin resolved to a browser stub that throws on
  first use, which no exit code will tell you about.
- **Unit-testing a module that imports `electron`.** Seed
  `require.cache[require.resolve('electron')]` with a fake before the
  first `require()` of the built module. Babel compiles a named
  import into a per-call-site `_electron.ipcRenderer` property read,
  so a getter in the fake's `exports` is re-consulted on every call
  and the stub can be swapped between tests. Keep shim modules on
  `import { ipcRenderer } from 'electron'` for this reason. Recipe in
  `test/AGENTS.md`.
- **depcheck sees `require.resolve('pkg')` in `vite.config.js`.** Use
  `createRequire(import.meta.url)` and `require.resolve()` for packages
  that only a bundler alias references, rather than a bare string
  replacement -- otherwise depcheck reports them unused and `npm test`
  fails, and the fix is not to add a depcheck ignore.
- **Vite replaces bare `process.env` with `{}`** unless
  `define: { 'process.env': 'globalThis.process.env' }` is set. Silent,
  and it kills startup at the first `path.join(process.env.HOME, ...)`
  inside `application-config-path`. Assume the same for any other
  Node-ism Vite thinks a browser cannot have.
- **The Vite entry filename is pinned** to `build-vite/renderer/main.js`
  via `output.entryFileNames`, because `static/main.html` references it
  from a plain `<script src>`. Do not restore hashed entry names.
- **Reading a renderer stack trace:** the `console-message` hook gives
  no stack. Temporarily add an inline
  `window.addEventListener('error', e => console.log('STACK:' +
  e.error.stack))` to `static/main.html` above the bundle tag and build
  with `npx vite build --minify false`.
- **Probing the rendered DOM:** hook `did-finish-load`, then
  `setTimeout(..., 4000)` before `wc.executeJavaScript` -- the event
  fires before React has rendered. Both windows fire it, so key on
  `location.pathname`.
- **`docs/vite-baseline.md`** is the measured inventory of what Vite
  cannot handle in the renderer, grouped by cause, and since US-014 the
  record of how each builtin was resolved. Consult it before guessing
  at scope for any Phase 1 or Phase 2 story.
- **`docs/platform-shim.md`** is the agreed shim contract from US-018:
  every exported function mapped onto the call sites it replaces, plus
  the shape changes each migration has to absorb (`stat.isDirectory()`
  becomes a boolean, `showOpenDialog` returns `[]` not `undefined` on
  cancel, no `createReadStream`). Read it before starting any US-020
  to US-034 story; do not re-derive the surface.
- **Shim conventions:** every export is `async` (FR-10), no channel
  string ever reaches a caller (one named function per channel), every
  listener registration resolves to an unsubscribe function and takes
  an optional `{ once: true }`. `shell.js` -- not `ipc-os.js` -- owns
  the `showItemInFolder`, `openPath` and `moveItemToTrash` channels,
  because they are one concern.
- **Migrating a call site to the async shim:** tail every promise with
  `.catch((err) => dispatch('error', err))`, the renderer's existing
  error sink, rather than making the caller `async` (that just moves
  the floating promise into React's or Electron's callback). Where the
  boundary cannot await at all -- a menu `click` -- extract a named
  helper. And cross-check `docs/platform-shim.md`'s call-site tables
  against the story's acceptance criteria first: the doc lists sites
  the criteria omit, and the story's own grep criterion covers them.
- **`test/unit/platform-boundary.js` is the migration regression
  gate.** It walks `src/renderer/**` (source, not `build/`, which has
  no `components/` or `controllers/`) and fails on any direct Electron
  use outside `lib/platform/`. Add each newly-migrated concern to its
  name list as US-027..US-032 land; US-034 replaces it with a lint
  rule.
- **A controller CAN be unit tested, by compiling it in the test.**
  `build:main` ignores `controllers/`, but `@babel/core`'s
  `transformFileSync(src, { root, cwd })` picks up the `babel` key in
  `package.json`, and writing the output into
  `build/renderer/controllers/` makes the controller's own
  `../lib/...` imports resolve against the real built
  `build/renderer/lib/**`. Stub the shims it imports by
  `require.cache`-seeding their **built** paths, and record dispatches
  via the real `lib/dispatcher`'s `setDispatch()`. This is the only
  way to assert controller behaviour instead of asserting on its
  source text -- see `test/unit/torrent-context-menu.js` and
  `test/AGENTS.md`.
- **`src/config.js` has a plain-`node` consumer now.** Since US-040 the
  engine sidecar requires it under `node`, so `electron` and
  `@electron/remote` are both required *lazily*, inside the branch of
  `getPath()` that needs them. Never hoist a host import to module
  scope in that file: `node_modules/electron` does not exist in a
  packaged sidecar, and `require('electron')` throws at load rather
  than returning `undefined`. The Vite renderer bundle is unaffected --
  a bare `require()` inside a function is still externalized, and the
  build keeps 0 externalization warnings.
- **`ws` is a direct dependency, pinned at 7.5.9** (it was already in
  the tree under `webtorrent`, so adding it deduped rather than
  installing a second copy). In 7.x the server export is
  `Server`, **not** `WebSocketServer` -- that alias is ws 8+. Also
  `new Server({ port })` starts listening immediately; there is no
  `listen()` call, so "wait for the port" means waiting on the
  `listening` event.
- **The sidecar runs as Electron in Node mode, not as `node`.** Since
  US-041 `src/main/engine-process.js` spawns `process.execPath` with
  `ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1`, so the packaged app ships no second
  runtime. In that mode `process.versions.electron` is set but
  `process.type` is undefined and `require('electron')` has no `app`,
  so any "am I in Electron?" test has to be on `process.type` --
  `getPath()` in `src/config.js` is the one that got this wrong. And
  `NODE_ENV=test` hides the whole class of bug: portable config never
  calls `getPath()`.
- **`pgrep -fl "engine/index.js"` is the launch check for the
  sidecar.** Its log output goes through `src/main/log.js` to the
  renderer devtools and never to the terminal; to read it, patch
  `engineProcess.init()` in `build/main/index.js` to pass a `log` that
  `console.log`s, then `npm run build` to restore. `kill -9` on that
  pid is how to prove the restart path in the real app.
- **Never `unref()` a retry/backoff timer.** During the delay it is
  often the only handle keeping the loop alive, so an unref'd one lets
  a bare Node process exit mid-backoff. In tape the symptom is "test
  exited without ending" for every remaining test in the run, which
  looks like a leak rather than an early exit.
- **`require('electron')` in a unit test may be a stub.** Other files
  seed `require.cache` for it and the tape process is shared, so the
  npm helper's path-string export is not dependable. Read the binary
  path from `node_modules/electron/path.txt` under
  `dirname(require.resolve('electron/package.json'))` plus a `dist`
  segment (`path.txt` omits it), and assert the file exists.
- **A source-scan test must drop comment lines first.** Scans for
  `window.`, `electron` or `.send(` match prose just as happily as
  code, and a file that *explains* what it no longer uses will fail its
  own check. Filter `/^\s*(\/\/|\*|\/\*)/` before matching -- see
  `test/unit/engine-entry.js`.

