## 2026-07-28 - US-027
- Migrated the `@electron/remote` dialog API and the two window-state
  reads onto the platform shim. Four call sites:
  - `components/path-selector.js`: `remote.dialog.showOpenDialogSync(
    remote.getCurrentWindow(), opts)` -> `platformDialog.showOpenDialog(
    opts)`. The `Array.isArray(filenames)` guard became a
    `filenames.length` check, per docs/platform-shim.md.
  - `controllers/subtitles-controller.js`: same conversion for
    `openSubtitles()`, plus a `dispatch('update')` in the continuation.
  - `controllers/torrent-list-controller.js`: `saveTorrentFileAs()`
    now uses `platformDialog.showSaveDialog(opts)`; the local
    `const win = remote.getCurrentWindow()` is gone.
  - `src/renderer/main.js`: the startup-sound `isVisible()` check and
    `setDimensions()`.
- `setDimensions()` was split. It now takes the four
  `window.screenX`/`outerWidth` geometry reads synchronously, *before*
  the `isMaximized()` suspension point, and hands them plus the single
  awaited boolean to a new `applyDimensions()` holding the rest of the
  old body in its original order. That is the resolution the shim
  design doc asked for. `applyDimensions()` ends with `update()`.
- Every shim promise is terminated with
  `.catch(err => dispatch('error', err))`; none float.
- `test/unit/platform-boundary.js` grew a second block of checks
  (dialog API, `getCurrentWindow().isVisible|isMaximized`). 4 failures
  before the migration -- exactly the 4 call sites -- 0 after. Full
  suite 287/287, `standard` clean.
- Files changed: `src/renderer/components/path-selector.js`,
  `src/renderer/controllers/subtitles-controller.js`,
  `src/renderer/controllers/torrent-list-controller.js`,
  `src/renderer/main.js`, `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`,
  `docs/platform-shim.md`, `test/AGENTS.md`,
  `src/renderer/controllers/AGENTS.md`, `specs/prd.json`.

**Deviation from the acceptance criteria, stated plainly.** "Zero
`@electron/remote` imports remain under `src/renderer/` outside
`lib/platform/`" is NOT met, and cannot be met by this story:
`controllers/torrent-list-controller.js` still imports `remote` for
`remote.Menu` / `remote.MenuItem` and one `remote.getCurrentWindow()`
inside `menu.popup()`. That is the context-menu concern, which is
US-029's whole scope. Doing it here would be doing US-029's work. The
boundary test therefore checks *concerns* (dialog, window-state reads)
rather than the `@electron/remote` specifier, and the grep goes to
zero when US-029 lands. Every other criterion is met except the manual
smoke test (see below).

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **Fixing `main.js:149` un-broke `delayedInit()`.** The
    `electron.remote.getCurrentWindow().isVisible()` call there had
    been throwing since Electron 14, aborting the rest of `onState()`.
    Two things are observably different now, both previously recorded
    in this file as known-dead: `console.timeEnd('init')` in `main.js`
    finally prints (the launch log now has *two* `init:` lines --
    `init: 25ms` from `build-vite/renderer/main.js` alongside the
    `webtorrent.js` one), and `delayedInit()` runs, so
    `checkDownloadPath()`, the visibility listener and `Cast.init()`
    all execute. Delete the "delayedInit is dead code" and
    "the init: line comes from webtorrent.js" entries from the
    patterns section -- they are now false.
  - **New pre-existing bug surfaced by that.** With `Cast.init()`
    running for the first time, the launch log gains a severity-3
    `Uncaught Error: no such type:
    extensions.api.cast_channel.CastMessage` from the bundled
    chromecast protobuf stack. It does not stop the app (init
    completes, the UI renders, probes work). It is dormant code being
    reached, not a regression from this story, and it is unowned by
    any current story -- likely a Vite-bundling casualty in
    `lib/cast.js`, whose deps were flagged as a deferred casualty in
    the shim AGENTS.md.
  - **Probing a *native* modal non-interactively.**
    `showOpenDialogSync` blocks the whole main process. So
    `wc.executeJavaScript("window.dispatch('openSubtitles')")` followed
    by any further `executeJavaScript` or `setTimeout` in the appended
    probe block produces *silence* -- and that silence is the
    evidence the dialog opened. Even `kill <pid>` does not land while
    the dialog is up; `pkill -f "$PWD/node_modules/"` is needed. Plan
    the blocking probe LAST in a launch, because nothing after it
    runs.
  - **`dispatch()` re-renders synchronously, shim calls do not.**
    `dispatch(action)` runs the handler then `update()` in the same
    turn, so state written inside a `.then()` misses that render.
    End the continuation with `dispatch('update')` (there is a no-op
    `update` handler for exactly this) unless it already dispatches
    something that re-renders. `path-selector.js` needs no such call:
    its `onChange` is `dispatch('updatePreferences', ...)`.
  - **Boundary tests should name concerns, not specifiers.** A file
    can hold two shim concerns owned by two different stories
    (`torrent-list-controller.js` holds dialog and menu). Banning the
    import specifier makes the story unpassable; banning
    `remote.dialog` and `getCurrentWindow().isMaximized` does not.
