## 2026-07-28 - US-034

Enforced the shim boundary with a real lint rule.

- `eslint-boundary.config.js` (new, repo root): a standalone ESLint
  config setting exactly one rule, `no-restricted-imports`, over
  `src/renderer/**/*.js`. Banned specifiers: `electron`,
  `@electron/remote`, `fs`, `os`, `crypto`, `http`, plus the
  `fs/promises`, `node:` and `original-fs` spellings of the same
  modules so the ban cannot be sidestepped by renaming the import.
  Parser is `@babel/eslint-parser`, the same one `standard` uses, so
  the rule parses exactly what `standard` parses -- JSX in `.js`
  included. Exemptions in override order: a directory glob for
  `src/renderer/lib/platform/**/*.js`, then exactly two per-file
  overrides for the deferred casualties `src/renderer/webtorrent.js`
  and `src/renderer/lib/cast.js`, each commented as such.
- `package.json`: new `lint:boundary` script
  (`eslint --no-eslintrc -c eslint-boundary.config.js src/renderer`);
  `lint` is now `standard && npm run lint:boundary`; `test` calls
  `npm run lint` instead of `standard` directly, so there is one
  source of truth. `eslint` 8.15.0 added as an explicit devDependency
  (it was already resolved at 8.15.0 as a transitive of `standard`;
  the lock diff is one line) and added to depcheck's `--ignores`.
- `test/unit/lint-boundary.js` (new, 46 assertions): drives the config
  through ESLint's Node API. Asserts every banned specifier is
  rejected with a message pointing at the shim, that the side-effect
  (`import 'fs'`) and re-export (`export {x} from 'fs'`) forms are
  caught too, that `path` / `react` / relative shim imports are still
  allowed, that all four exempt paths (including a not-yet-existing
  new shim module) may import freely, that files outside
  `src/renderer/` are untouched by the config, that exactly two
  per-file overrides exist, and that the real renderer tree lints
  clean.
- `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`: added a `require()` counterpart
  check over the same specifier list, and rewrote the header comment.
- Docs: new "Enforcing the boundary" section in
  `docs/platform-shim.md`; `test/AGENTS.md` and
  `src/renderer/lib/platform/AGENTS.md` updated.

### Acceptance criteria

All seven met. AC-5 verified literally: added `import os from 'os'` as
line 1 of `src/renderer/lib/dispatcher.js`, ran `npm test`, and the run
stopped at `lint:boundary` with `'os' import is restricted from being
used. The renderer must not reach Node builtins directly. Import it
from src/renderer/lib/platform/ instead (FR-11)` -- before `depcheck`
or the unit tests executed. Reverted; boundary lint back to exit 0.
AC-6 ("no other eslint or standard setting is changed") holds: the
`standard` key in `package.json` is untouched and the new config is
run with `--no-eslintrc`.

`npm test` exits 0: standard, lint:boundary, depcheck, 654/654 unit
assertions. No file under `src/` changed, so FR-2 is unaffected; the
launch check was run anyway and reports `init: 922.1ms` with no
leftover processes.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- **`standard` has no `rules` escape hatch.** Its config key accepts
  only `ignore`, `globals`, `env`, `parser`, `plugins`, `settings`.
  Every project-specific rule needs a second standalone ESLint run.
  Consolidated at the top.
- **US-034 does NOT supersede `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`,**
  despite what `test/AGENTS.md` and that file's own header comment
  predicted before this session. `no-restricted-imports` sees `import`
  declarations only -- it cannot see `require('fs')`, `ipcRenderer.`
  member reads, `wt-` channel strings, or `new remote.Menu`. Deleting
  the source scan would have been a straight loss of coverage, so it
  stayed and grew a `require()` check instead. Both stale comments
  were corrected. The story's acceptance criteria never asked for the
  deletion; that was a forward guess made in US-026.
- **Order matters when probing a lint failure.** The first attempt at
  the AC-5 verification appended `import os from 'os'` to the *end* of
  `dispatcher.js`, and `standard` failed first on `import/first` --
  which proves nothing about the new rule. Put the probe import on
  line 1 so `standard` passes and the boundary rule is unambiguously
  the thing that failed.
- **ESLint's Node API is the right way to test a config.**
  `lintText(source, { filePath })` lints a path that need not exist,
  so exemption coverage can be asserted for a hypothetical future shim
  module without creating a file under `src/` that a crashed run would
  leave behind. Consolidated at the top.
- **`depcheck` does not credit a devDependency used only from an npm
  script.** `standard` and `@babel/eslint-parser` were already in
  `--ignores` for that reason; `eslint` joins them. Adding a
  tool-only devDependency means editing that list in the same commit
  or `npm test` fails on the next line.
