## 2026-07-29 - US-041
- Added `src/main/engine-process.js`: the host-side supervisor for the
  engine sidecar. `createEngineProcess(options)` spawns the built engine
  entry point, parses the single `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PORT=` line off its
  stdout, forwards every other stdout line to `log()` and every stderr
  line to `log.error()`, restarts the child with doubling backoff
  (500ms -> 8s, five attempts) when it exits unexpectedly, and logs
  "giving up" at the cap. `init({ app })` wires the singleton to
  `before-quit`, `will-quit` and `process.on('exit')`; `getEndpoint()`
  returns `{ url, token }` or `null`, held in the main process and ready
  for US-042 to expose through the shim. No `wt-*` message passes
  through this file (ADR-012).
- The child is spawned as **Electron in Node mode**
  (`ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1`), so the packaged app needs no second
  runtime. That exposed a real bug in `src/config.js`: `getPath()`
  tested `process.versions.electron`, which is set in Node mode, so the
  sidecar took the main-process branch and died on
  `require('electron').app.getPath` at load. It now branches on
  `process.type` (`'renderer'` / `'browser'` / anything else is plain
  Node).
- Added the orphan backstop: with `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PARENT_PIPE=1` the
  sidecar shuts down when its stdin pipe closes, which covers a host
  killed without `before-quit` ever running. Gated behind the flag
  because a hand-run sidecar gets `/dev/null` on stdin and would exit
  immediately.
- Wired `engineProcess.init()` into `src/main/index.js`'s `onReady`,
  after the windows. The hidden window is still the live engine until
  US-045, so a start failure is logged rather than fatal.
- Tests, written first and confirmed failing (module not found):
  `test/unit/engine-process.js`, 38 assertions, all against the real
  child process rather than a fake `child_process` -- endpoint shape and
  loopback url, token accepted / wrong token refused by the real child,
  stdout through `log` and stderr through `log.error`, the app's actual
  Electron-as-Node configuration, a hand-`SIGKILL`ed child restarting on
  a new port with the restart logged, the restart cap (spawn count
  exactly `1 + maxRestarts`, `start()` rejects, nothing scheduled
  after), `kill()` leaving no orphan and no respawn, `init()`'s
  `before-quit` listener killing the child, and the parent-pipe
  shutdown.
- Files changed: `src/main/engine-process.js` (new),
  `test/unit/engine-process.js` (new), `src/main/index.js`,
  `src/engine/index.js`, `src/config.js`, `src/AGENTS.md`,
  `src/engine/AGENTS.md`, `progress.log`, `specs/prd.json`.
- Verification: full suite green, 1574 assertions (up from 1535),
  `npm run lint` clean, `npm run build` clean with 0 externalization
  warnings. One launch check (`npx electron --no-sandbox .`, probe
  appended to `build/main/index.js` to route the engine log to the
  terminal, killed afterwards, `pgrep` clean): `init: 838ms`, the
  sidecar came up as a child of Electron and printed its port,
  `kill -9` on it produced `exited with code null, signal SIGKILL` then
  `restarting in 500ms (attempt 1 of 5)` and a new pid, and quitting the
  app left no engine process behind. **The six-step manual smoke test
  was not run** (non-interactive session); nothing was cut over, and the
  residual risk is the `config.js` `getPath()` change, which the launch
  check exercises on the main-process branch and the new unit test
  exercises on the Node-mode branch.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **`ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE` is how the sidecar gets a Node runtime, and
    it is not the same environment as `node`.** `process.versions.electron`
    is set, `process.type` is undefined, and `require('electron')`
    resolves to a module with no `app`. Any "am I in Electron?" test
    written the obvious way sends that process down the main-process
    branch. Branch on `process.type` instead. US-047 inherits this: the
    packaged sidecar is the same binary as the app.
  - **`NODE_ENV=test` hides the config path bug.** It makes config
    portable, and portable never calls `getPath()`. A test that spawns
    something which loads `config` and wants to prove the *paths*
    resolve has to run outside `NODE_ENV=test` -- with the compensating
    argument that the engine only reads those paths at startup and
    writes nothing under them until commanded.
  - **Do not `unref()` a restart timer.** During the backoff it is the
    only handle keeping the loop alive; unref'd, a bare Node process
    (a unit test, or the sidecar driven by hand) exits mid-backoff
    instead of restarting, and the symptom is tape reporting "test
    exited without ending" for every remaining test in the run.
  - **`require('electron')` is not reliable in a unit test that runs in
    the full suite.** Other files seed `require.cache` for `electron`
    with a stub and one tape process is shared, so the npm helper's
    path string may already be gone. Read the binary path directly:
    `path.join(dirname(require.resolve('electron/package.json')),
    'dist', readFileSync('.../path.txt'))` -- note the `dist` segment,
    which `path.txt` does not include. Assert the file exists, or a
    wrong path looks exactly like a sidecar that cannot start.
  - **The supervisor is testable end to end without Electron**, because
    the electron import is lazy and inside `init()` and `app` is
    injectable. Prefer that to `require.cache`-seeding `electron`: it
    leaks across files, and the fake `app` here is four lines.
  - **The engine's log output is invisible in a launch check** -- it
    goes through `src/main/log.js` to the renderer devtools. To see it,
    patch `engineProcess.init()` in `build/main/index.js` to pass a
    `log` that `console.log`s, launch, then `npm run build` to restore.
    `pgrep -fl "engine/index.js"` is the cheaper check for "is the
    sidecar up", and its pid is what you `kill -9` to prove the restart
    path in the real app.
