## 2026-07-29 - US-042

Expose the engine endpoint through the shim.

**What was implemented**

- `src/renderer/lib/platform/ipc-os.js` gains `getEngineEndpoint()`,
  `async` like every other export, resolving with `{ url, token }` as
  plain data or `null`. It is a `send` plus an `ipcRenderer.once` on
  the `getEngineEndpoint` channel -- the same request/response shape
  `checkForExternalPlayer` already uses, rather than
  `invoke`/`handle`, so the existing fake `ipcRenderer` in the unit
  tests covers it unchanged and the main side stays in the one style
  `src/main/ipc.js` uses throughout.
- `src/main/ipc.js` answers that channel from
  `engineProcess.waitForEndpoint()`. Nothing but the two strings
  crosses the boundary (ADR-007). The reply is guarded by
  `isDestroyed()` because the window can go away during the wait.
- `src/main/engine-process.js` gains `waitForEndpoint()` at both the
  module and the supervisor level. It resolves with the *current*
  endpoint, waiting when there is none: the renderer may ask before
  the first spawn has bound a port, or during the gap while a restart
  is in flight. Waiters are per gap (a fresh array flushed in
  `onPort`), not the single `started` promise, so a wait that spans a
  restart resolves with the new port rather than the dead one.
  `kill()` resolves pending waiters with `null` and the give-up path
  rejects them, so no request can hang forever.
- `docs/platform-shim.md`: `getEngineEndpoint` added to the `ipc-os.js`
  command table with the endpoint semantics.

**Tests** (written first, all four failing before the implementation)

- `test/unit/platform-ipc-os.js`: the endpoint round trip, the `null`
  reply, listener cleanup, and one request per call so the port after a
  restart is what the second call sees. `getEngineEndpoint` joins
  `checkForExternalPlayer` in the `skip` list of the wt-* test.
- `test/unit/engine-process.js`: `waitForEndpoint()` resolving for a
  caller that arrived before the port line, resolving with the *new*
  port after a `SIGKILL` restart while keeping the same token,
  rejecting once the supervisor gives up, and the module-level form
  answering `null` with nothing supervised.
- `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`: AC-4 as a static scan -- no
  renderer file, *including the shim itself*, reads the endpoint from
  the sidecar environment variables or a `window`/`globalThis` engine
  global. The walk deliberately includes `lib/platform/`, because the
  shim is the file that would be tempted, and comment lines are
  dropped first.

**Verification**

- `npm test` -- 1641 assertions, all passing.
- `npm run lint` clean.
- One launch check with a temporary probe appended to
  `build/main/index.js` (removed by `npm run build` afterwards): both
  renderer windows sent `getEngineEndpoint` over the real channel and
  logged back `{"url":"ws://127.0.0.1:58646","token":"0cf5c..."}`.
  That is the main-process handler proven end to end, which no unit
  test can reach. No smoke test was run (non-interactive session);
  nothing in the UI calls the new export yet, so there is no
  user-visible surface to smoke until US-044.

**Learnings for future iterations**

- **`src/main/ipc.js` cannot be unit tested** -- it requires
  `electron`'s `app`/`ipcMain` at module scope and every handler
  reaches for a sibling main-process module. The pattern that does
  work: unit test the module the handler delegates to (here
  `engine-process.waitForEndpoint()`), then prove the wiring with one
  launch probe that calls `ipcRenderer.send` from
  `wc.executeJavaScript` and reads the reply back through the
  `console-message` hook. Both renderer windows answer, which is
  itself useful evidence the handler is not window-specific.
- **`e.sender.send` needs an `isDestroyed()` guard** whenever the reply
  is asynchronous. Any handler that awaits before replying can outlive
  its window, and sending to a destroyed `webContents` throws inside
  the promise chain where nothing catches it.
- **A "wait for a value that changes" API needs per-gap waiters, not
  one promise.** `start()` resolves once with the first endpoint;
  reusing it for later callers would hand out the port of a process
  that has already died. An array of `{resolve, reject}` flushed each
  time the value arrives is the shape, and every terminal path (kill,
  give up) has to flush it too or a caller hangs forever.
